  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 312 AM CDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nHigh pressure is building southward across the central US this\nmorning. Through the day, this high will settle into the Middle\nMississippi Valley to the Great Lakes area. This will shift our\nwinds from light and variable, to the east, and eventually to \nthe southeast late this afternoon and evening. Temperatures \noverperformed my forecast by a few degrees on Sunday and with \nclear skies and light winds shifting to the southeast, we should\nonce again see temperatures climb into the middle to upper 70s.\n\nFor Tuesday onward, high pressure shifts to the eastern US, with low-\nlevel flow becoming southerly to southwesterly. In the upper-levels, \nbroad upper ridging builds over the western US. This will lead to \nwarm advection through the middle of the country and as a result, \nwe'll see our highs climb into the low to mid 80s for the southern \ntwo-thirds of the forecast area, and the upper 70s for northern \nMissouri to the Iowa state line. This places highs about 10-15\ndegrees above normal for this time of year. This pattern is \nalso very likely to keep the area dry through most of the \nforecast. The exception continues to look like Thursday night. \nEnsemble guidance depicts a broad low chance, about 15-20% for \nat least 0.01\" of rain, across northern Missouri. Overnight, \nmodels show an area of ascent, noted from 300k to 305K as the \nlow-level jet develops. This also feeds into an area of \ndiffluence aloft with strong upper-level jet energy moving \nacross Iowa. Moisture still seems fairly limited though, with \nGFS forecast soundings for northern Missouri and southern Iowa \nshowing the lowest 10,000 feet being dry, with some elevated \ninstability above this dry layer. For now, slight chance PoPs \nare reasonable given the offsetting parameters of modest ascent\nbut relatively poor low-level moisture profiles.",
